1. Summary and Technical Explanation

While testing the application, I found that the password reset functionality at /forgot-password does not invalidate the reset token after it has been successfully used. A single reset token can be redeemed multiple times, and each use changes the account password again.

Reset tokens should be single-use. After a successful password change, the server must mark the token as consumed or delete it so it can never be replayed. In this case the server validates only that the token exists and has not expired. It does not track whether the token has already been redeemed. As a result, the token remains a valid credential for the account long after the legitimate user has finished the reset flow.

This means any party who obtains a previously used token can take over the account. Tokens can leak through email account compromise, browser history, proxy or server logs, Referer headers, shared or monitored devices, or a forwarded email. Because the token keeps working, the legitimate password change does not close the window.

2. Steps to Reproduce
Create a new account on the application.
Navigate to https://geo-salmon-complement-walk.trycloudflare.com/forgot-password.
Enter the account’s email address and request a password reset.
Open the reset link received and set the new password to 1234566.
Confirm that the password change succeeds and that login with 1234566 works.
Open the same reset link again (the same token, without requesting a new one).
Set the password to password12.
Observe that the server accepts the request and changes the password again.
Log in with password12. The login succeeds.

Expected result: The token is rejected at step 6 as invalid, expired, or already used.

Actual result: The token is accepted again and remains valid, allowing unlimited password resets with the same token.

3. Impact
Account takeover: An attacker who obtains any previously used reset token can set a new password and log in as the victim, even after the victim has already completed their own reset.
Persistent access: The attacker can keep re-using the token to regain access even if the victim changes their password again, until the token expires (if it expires at all).
Increased exposure window: Tokens leaked through email compromise, logs, browser history, or referrer leakage stay exploitable for much longer than they should.
Undermined recovery flow: Users assume that completing a reset secures their account. With this flaw, the reset link effectively becomes a long-lived credential.
4. Remediation
Enforce single use: Invalidate or delete the reset token immediately after a successful password change, in the same database transaction as the password update.
Short expiration: Keep token lifetime short (e.g., 15 to 30 minutes) and enforce expiry server-side.
Invalidate older tokens: When a new reset is requested, invalidate all previously issued tokens for that account.
Invalidate on credential changes: Revoke outstanding reset tokens whenever the user changes their password or email through any other flow.
Terminate active sessions: After a password reset, invalidate existing sessions and refresh tokens for the account.
Store tokens securely: Generate tokens with a cryptographically secure random generator (at least 128 bits of entropy) and store only a hash of the token in the database.
Notify the user: Send a confirmation email after a password change so the owner can detect unauthorized resets.
Reduce leakage: Set Referrer-Policy: no-referrer on the reset page and avoid loading third-party resources there.
